2026-08-26T06:23:04Z info  argv: pr create --repo mas-bandwidth/rocketnet --draft --head outside-in-header --title The outside-in contract: rocketnet.h for review, plus README --body The public header rewritten against today's full ruling set, for Glenn's review before any implementation follows it. Draft on purpose: the .cpp and tests deliberately lag this header until the interface survives review, so CI is expected red here.\n\nWhat the header now says, mapped to the rulings:\n\nThe caller sends packets and the wire carries transport packets; the vocabulary section at the top settles it once. Channels are gone entirely. There are exactly three packet types, always present on every connection, chosen per packet at send time: ROCKETNET_PACKET_RELIABLE (in order), ROCKETNET_PACKET_LATEST (reliable, most recent only), ROCKETNET_PACKET_UNRELIABLE. Nothing to configure, nothing for two ends to agree on beyond max_packet_bytes and protocol version.\n\nEvery server send takes one uint64 destination. High bit clear, the low bits are a client index; high bit set, a flow id. Constructors rocketnet_destination_client and rocketnet_destination_flow build them. The same argument works on start_packet, the batch form, and the copy convenience, so unicast and broadcast are the same line of code.\n\nServer peers are client indices, plain ints in the netcode slot model. Flows are created and destroyed on the server only, subscription by client index with batch forms, and a client receiving a packet sees no flow identity and no type argument, because by delivery the discipline already happened.\n\nThe transport is the swappable seam: SOCKET, GSO, IO_URING, XDP, IOCP, RIO, KQUEUE, plus CUSTOM as a caller-supplied function table, which makes the loss and reorder soak harness just another transport. Each transport reports properties, the core derives its splitting from the reported MTU, ROCKETNET_MIN_TRANSPORT_MTU is 1400 with the headroom reserved for framing and tricks, and a transport under the floor is refused at create. ROCKETNET_SMALL_PACKET_BYTES 1280 is the size guaranteed to never fragment on any conforming transport.\n\nThe zero copy send ceremony survives unchanged in shape: start_packet, write in place, send_packet, with the pool, quota, generation counter and threading contracts carried over from the previous header.\n\nREADME.md is new: the model on one page, status honest about the implementation lagging.\n\nOpen questions flagged in place for review: head of line blocking within one destination on RELIABLE (stream ids only if a real workload asks), and the flow subscribe-mid-stream ordering contract.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)
